"Deletion Analysis of the Escherichia coli Taurine and Alkanesulfonate Transport Systems | Journal of Bacteriology (https://journals.asm.org/doi/10.1128/jb.182.10.2687-2695.2000)\nciteturn31view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://journals.asm.org/doi/10.1128/jb.182.10.2687-2695.2000\",\"lineno\":83}); Total lines: 728\nL0: 【0†iframe†www.googletagmanager.com】\nL1: \nL2: 【1†CLOSE】\nL3: \nL4:   * 【2†This Journal】\nL5:   * 【3†Anywhere】\nL6: \nL7: Enter words / phrases / DOI / ISBN / keywords / authors / etc[Input: Search here within this Journal][Input]\nL8: \nL9: Search Search\nL10: \nL11: Enter words / phrases / DOI / ISBN / keywords / authors / etc[Input: Enter words / phrases / DOI / ISBN / keywords / authors / etc]\nL12: \nL13: Search Search\nL14: \nL15: 【4†Advanced search】\nL16: \nL17: Suggested Terms:\nL18:   * 【5†open access policy】\nL19:   * 【6†peer review at ASM】\nL20:   * 【7†what it costs to publish with ASM】\nL21:   * 【8†calls for editors】\nL22:   * 【9†asm journals collections】\nL23: \nL24: 【10†Skip to main content】\nL25: \nL26: 【1†BACK】\nL27:   * 【11†Antimicrobial Agents and Chemotherapy】\nL28:   * 【12†Applied and Environmental Microbiology】\nL29:   * 【13†ASM Animal Microbiology】\nL30:   * 【14†ASM Case Reports】\nL31:   * 【15†ASM Food Microbiology】\nL32:   * 【16†Clinical Microbiology Reviews】\nL33:   * 【17†Infection and Immunity】\nL34:   * 【18†Journal of Bacteriology】\nL35:   * 【19†Journal of Clinical Microbiology】\nL36:   * 【20†Journal of Microbiology & Biology Education】\nL37:   * 【21†Journal of Virology】\nL38:   * 【22†mBio】\nL39:   * 【23†Microbiology and Molecular Biology Reviews】\nL40:   * 【24†Microbiology Resource Announcements】\nL41:   * 【25†Microbiology Spectrum】\nL42:   * 【26†mSphere】\nL43:   * 【27†mSystems】\nL44:   * 【28†Shopping cart with 0 item】\nL45: 【29†Login / Register】\nL46: \nL47: 【30†Skip main navigation】\nL48:   *   * 【18†JOURNAL HOME】\nL49:   * 【1†FOR AUTHORS】\nL50:   * 【1†ARTICLES】\nL51:     *     * 【31†CURRENT ISSUE】\nL52:     *     * 【32†LATEST ARTICLES】\nL53:     * 【33†COLLECTIONS】\nL54:     * 【34†ARCHIVE】\nL55:   * 【1†ABOUT THE JOURNAL】\nL56:     *     * 【35†ABOUT JB】\nL57:     *     * 【36†SUBMIT】\nL58:     * 【37†SCOPE】\nL59:     * 【38†EDITORIAL BOARD】\nL60:     * 【39†ARTICLE TYPES AND FORMATTING】\nL61:     * 【40†SUBMISSION AND REVIEW PROCESS】\nL62:     * 【41†ACTIVE CONTRIBUTOR TRACK】\nL63:     * 【42†FOR REVIEWERS】\nL64:     * 【43†FAQ】\nL65:     * 【44†CONTACT US】\nL66:   * 【1†FOR SUBSCRIBERS】\nL67:     *     * 【45†MEMBERS】\nL68:     *     * 【46†INSTITUTIONS】\nL69:     * 【47†ALERTS】\nL70:     * 【48†RSS】\nL71: Research Article\nL72: \nL73: 15 May 2000\nL74: # Deletion Analysis of the Escherichia coli Taurine and Alkanesulfonate Transport Systems\nL75: \nL76: Authors: 【49†Eric Eichhorn】, 【50†Jan R. van der Ploeg】, 【51†Thomas Leisinger】【52†Authors Info & Affiliations】\nL77: \nL78: 【53†https://doi.org/10.1128/jb.182.10.2687-2695.2000†doi.org】\nL79: \nL80: 【54†Cite】\nL81: \nL82: 【55†PDF/EPUB】\nL83: ## ABSTRACT\nL84: The Escherichia coli tauABCD and ssuEADCBgene clusters are required for the utilization of taurine and alkanesulfonates as sulfur sources and are expressed only under conditions of sulfate or cysteine starvation. tauD andssuD encode an α-ketoglutarate-dependent taurine dioxygenase and a reduced flavin mononucleotide-dependent alkanesulfonate monooxygenase, respectively. These enzymes are responsible for the desulfonation of taurine and alkanesulfonates.\nL85: The amino acid sequences of SsuABC and TauABC exhibit similarity to those of components of the ATP-binding cassette transporter superfamily, suggesting that two uptake systems for alkanesulfonates are present inE. coli. Chromosomally located in-frame deletions of thetauABC and ssuABC genes were constructed inE. coli strain EC1250, and the growth properties of the mutants were studied to investigate the requirement for the TauABC and SsuABC proteins for growth on alkanesulfonates as sulfur sources.\nL86: Complementation analysis of in-frame deletion mutants confirmed that the growth phenotypes obtained were the result of the in-frame deletions constructed. The range of substrates transported by these two uptake systems was largely reflected in the substrate specificities of the TauD and SsuD desulfonation systems. However, certain known substrates of TauD were transported exclusively by the SsuABC system.\nL87: Mutants in which only formation of hybrid transporters was possible were unable to grow with sulfonates, indicating that the individual components of the two transport systems were not functionally exchangeable. The TauABCD and SsuEADCB systems involved in alkanesulfonate uptake and desulfonation thus are complementary to each other at the levels of both transport and desulfonation.\nL88: In Escherichia coli, sulfate starvation causes increased synthesis of several proteins involved in scavenging sulfur from alternative sulfur sources (【56†15】). Recently, two sets of genes whose expression is derepressed in the absence of sulfate or cysteine were identified. ThetauABCD gene cluster, located at 8.5 min on the E. coli chromosome, encodes a sulfonate-sulfur utilization system that is specifically involved in the utilization of taurine (2-aminoethanesulfonic acid) as a source of sulfur.\nL89: Disruption oftauB, tauC, or tauD resulted in the loss of the ability to utilize taurine as a source of sulfur but did not affect the utilization of a range of other aliphatic sulfonates (【57†21】). The TauD protein is an α-ketoglutarate-dependent taurine dioxygenase (【58†3】), and the TauABC proteins exhibit similarity to ATP-binding cassette (ABC)-type transport systems (【57†21】). A second set of genes, the ssuEADCB gene cluster, located at 21.4 min on the chromosome, enables E.\nL90: coli to utilize aliphatic sulfonates other than taurine as a source of sulfur. Deletion of ssuEADCB caused an inability to utilize alkanesulfonates but did not affect the utilization of taurine (【59†24】).\nL91: SsuD is a monooxygenase that catalyzes the desulfonation of a wide range of sulfonated substrates other than taurine, including C_{2} to C_{10} unsubstituted linear alkanesulfonates, substituted ethanesulfonic acids and the buffer substances HEPES, MOPS (morpholinepropanesulfonic acid), and PIPES [piperazine-N,N′-bis(2-ethanesulfonic acid)]. This monooxygenase is dependent on reduced flavin mononucleotide (FMN) which is provided by the SsuE protein, an NAD(P)H:FMN oxidoreductase (【60†4】).\nL92: The SsuABC proteins also appear to constitute an ABC transport system (【59†24】). Neither the TauD enzyme nor the two-component SsuD-SsuE monooxygenase desulfonated methanesulfonic acid, cysteic acid, or aromatic sulfonates (【60†4】), compounds that are unable to satisfy the sulfur requirement of E. coliEC1250. These two enzyme systems thus cover the full range of desulfonation activities in this E. coli strain.\nL93: They convert alkanesulfonates to the corresponding aldehyde and sulfite, which has been shown to enter the sulfite reduction pathway to cysteine (【61†20】).\nL94: In the present study we investigated the role of the tauABCand ssuABC genes in the utilization of taurine and alkanesulfonates as sulfur sources. The tauA andssuA genes encode putative signal sequences, indicating that their products probably function as periplasmic binding proteins. The sequences of TauB and SsuB and of TauC and SsuC are significantly similar to those of ATP-binding proteins and integral membrane components, respectively, of members of the ABC transporter superfamily (【62†6】).\nL95: By analogy to known binding-protein-dependent ABC transporters (【63†2】), it is inferred that these systems are composed of a homodimeric membrane protein and a homodimeric ATP-binding protein. A pairwise comparison of the components of the TauABC and SsuABC transporters revealed sequence identities of 22.7% for TauA and SsuA, 40.4% for TauB and SsuB, and 34.5% for TauC and SsuC.\nL96: Using a genetic approach, we explored to what extent the substrate specificity of the TauD and SsuD-SsuE desulfonation systems is reflected in the substrate range of the corresponding transport systems and whether components of the two transport systems are functionally exchangeable.\nL97: ## MATERIALS AND METHODS\nL98: \nL99: ### Chemicals.\nL100: \nL101: All chemicals used as sulfur sources were of the highest quality available and were obtained from Fluka, exceptN-phenyltaurine and 4-phenyl-1-butanesulfonate (Sigma), isethionic acid (Aldrich), and 3-aminopropanesulfonate (Acros Organics). Oligonucleotides were purchased from Microsynth (Balgach, Switzerland). Restriction endonucleases, T4 DNA ligase, andTaq DNA polymerase were obtained from MBI Fermentas.Pfu DNA polymerase was from Promega.\nL102: ### E. coli strains and growth conditions.\nL103: E. coli strain DH5α (【64†16】), used for cloning purposes, was grown with constant shaking (180 rpm) at 37 or 30°C in Luria-Bertani (LB) medium (【64†16】). Solid media were prepared by addition of 1.5% (wt/vol) agar. When appropriate, the following additions were made: ampicillin, 100 μg/ml; kanamycin, 50 μg/ml; chloramphenicol, 35 μg/ml; isopropyl-β-d-1-thiogalactopyranoside (IPTG), 0.5 mM; 5-bromo-4-chloro-3-indolyl galactoside (X-Gal), 80 μg/ml; and sucrose, 5% (wt/vol).\nL104: For plasmid isolation, restriction enzyme digestion, and transformation of E. coli, standard procedures were used (【65†1】). DNA for sequencing was prepared using either the QiaSpin Miniprep kit from Qiagen or the Jetstar Midiprep kit from Genomed.\nL105: E. coli EC1250 (MC4100 trp-1) (【66†7】) and the corresponding deletion mutants of this strain were grown at 37°C in a sulfur-free M63 medium (【57†21】) supplemented with 4 μg of tryptophan per ml and the desired sulfur source at 250 μM. Growth curves were determined in microtiter plates with 150 μl of culture by using a SPECTRAmax Plus microtiter plate reader with SOFTmax PRO software (Molecular Devices).\nL106: Overnight cultures grown in sulfur-free M63 minimal medium supplemented with sulfate as a sulfur source were diluted 100-fold in sulfur-free M63 minimal medium, and then 75-μl portions of the diluted overnight cultures were pipetted into the wells of a microtiter plate containing 75 μl of minimal medium supplemented with the appropriate sulfur source at a concentration of 500 μM. The optical density at 600 nm was measured every 5 min.\nL107: The plate was shaken for 30 s before every measurement to ensure aerobic growth conditions.\nL108: ### Construction of chromosomal in-frame deletion mutants.\nL109: The DNA (at least 500 bp) flanking the gene or group of genes to be deleted was amplified using Pfu DNA polymerase. Oligonucleotide primers were designed to introduce adequate restriction sites for subsequent cloning purposes (Table 【67†1】). Their approximate locations in the tau and ssuoperons are shown in Fig. 【68†1】.\nL110: Identical restriction sites were introduced at the 5′ end (around 20 bp downstream of the start codon) and at the 3′ end (30 to 40 bp before the stop codon) of the gene or group of genes to be deleted. The external primers used for PCR of the flanking regions introduced restriction sites available in plasmid pBluescript II KS (Stratagene). After digestion with the appropriate restriction enzymes, both PCR products were ligated together into pBluescript.\nL111: The inserts of the resulting plasmids were sequenced to confirm that in-frame ligation had occurred and that no changes in the DNA sequence were introduced during PCR. Subsequently the deletion inserts were subcloned in plasmid pKO3 (【69†10】). All plasmids used for the construction of chromosomalE. coli EC1250 in-frame deletion mutations of tauand ssu genes are listed in Table【70†2】.\nL112: Table 1.\nL113: \nL114: Table 1. Oligonucleotide primers used for the construction oftau and ssu deletions\nL115: Primer  | Sequence【71†^{a}】  | Nucleotide position