oducts fortauD deletion in PstI/HindIII pBluescript  | This study\nL197: pME4721  | pKO3 withNotI-SalI insert from pME4720 for tauDdeletion  | This study\nL198: pME4722  | PstI-HindIII +HindIII-XhoI PCR products for ssuDdeletion in PstI/XhoI pBluescript  | This study\nL199: pME4723  | pKO3 with NotI-SalI insert from pME4722 for ssuD deletion  | This study\nL200: pME4724  | NdeI-HindIIItauABCD insert from pUC18ALA4 inNdeI/HindIII pUC19  | This study\nL201: pME4725  | NdeI-SphI tauABC insert from pUC18ALA4 in NdeI/SphI pUC19  | This study\nL202: pME4726  | NdeI-BamHI tauAB insert from pME4724 in NdeI/BamHI pUC19  | This study\nL203: pME4727  | EcoRV-HindIII (blunt) deletion in pME4724  | This study\nL204: pME4728  | EcoRI-ClaI ssuEA insert from pME4221 in EcoRI/ClaI pBluescript  | This study\nL205: pME4729  | EcoRI-SalI ssuEADinsert from pME4221 in EcoRI/SalI pBluescript  | This study\nL206: pME4730  | StuI-SmaI deletion in pME4221  | This study\nL207: pME4731  | HincII deletion in pME4724  | This study\nL208: pME4732  | NdeI-HindIIItauABCD insert from pME4724 inNdeI/HindIII pET-24a(+)  | This study\nL209: pME4733  | NdeI-EcoRV tauA insert from pME4724 in NdeI/HindIII (blunt) pET-24a(+)  | This study\nL210: pME4734  | BamHI (blunt)-HindIII (blunt) deletion in pME4732  | This study\nL211: pME4821  | 450-bp BamHI-EcoRItau promoter region from pUC18ALA4 inBamHI/EcoRI pUC19  | This study\nL212: pME4822  | 340-bp BamHI-EcoRI ssupromoter region from pME4204 in BamHI/EcoRI pUC19  | This study\nL213: In-frame deletion mutants of E. coli EC1250 were constructed as described by Link et al. (【69†10】) with some modifications.E. coli EC1250 was first transformed with a pKO3 derivative by using heat shock or electroporation (【65†1】). Transformants were selected at 30°C on LB plates containing chloramphenicol. On the second day, five to seven chloramphenicol-resistant colonies were resuspended in 0.5 ml of LB medium and diluted 10^{2}-, 10^{3}-, and 10^{4}-fold.\nL214: A portion (300 to 500 μl) of each dilution was plated onto prewarmed LB-chloramphenicol plates and incubated at 43°C overnight. On the third day, five to seven chloramphenicol-resistant colonies were resuspended and diluted as described above, plated on LB-sucrose plates, and incubated at 30°C overnight. On the fourth day, 100 to 500 colonies from the LB-sucrose plates were picked on LB-sucrose and LB-sucrose-chloramphenicol plates and incubated at 30°C overnight.\nL215: When possible, an additional screening on sulfur-free M63 minimal medium plates was done to find out which of the positive colonies (sucrose resistant and chloramphenicol sensitive) were unable to grow with taurine or butanesulfonate as a sulfur source and thus were likely to carry the desired deletion. Putative deletion mutants were analyzed using colony PCR withTaq DNA polymerase.\nL216: A single colony was resuspended into 20 μl of sterile deionized water, from which 1 μl was added to a 50-μl PCR mixture (【65†1】). The sequence of interest was amplified using the external primers used for the construction of the deletion; PCR products were analyzed on 0.8% TAE-agarose gels. Positive clones were purified on LB plates at least three times, checking each time for the presence of the desired in-frame deletion by colony PCR.\nL217: For the construction of multiple EC1250 deletion mutants, a deletion mutant was subjected to another round of deletion as described above. Overall, the frequency of in-frame deletions obtained in successful experiments amounted to 1.4% of the putatively positive clones that were screened.\nL218: ### Construction of tauD and ssuD tauDmutants.\nL219: Since efforts to obtain an in-frame deletion oftauD remained unsuccessful, a tauD mutant ofE. coli EC1250 was constructed by P1 transduction (【77†11】). The tauD::lacZ fusion of the E. coli MC4100 derivative MW108 [Φ(tauD::lacZ)(λplacMu9)] (【57†21】) was transduced into E. coli EC1250.\nL220: Kanamycin-resistant transductants were screened for inability to grow on M63 minimal medium with taurine as a sulfur source and for the formation of blue colonies on M63 minimal medium supplemented with glutathione as a sulfur source and X-Gal to confirm the presence of thelacZ fusion. To obtain an ssuD tauD double mutant, an EC1250 mutant with ssuD deleted was used as a recipient.\nL221: ### Complementation analysis of in-frame deletion mutants.\nL222: Restriction sites which are unique within the tauABCD andssuEADCB operons were used to construct the plasmids shown in Fig. 【68†1】 and listed in Table 【70†2】. Plasmids for the complementation analysis of tau deletions were constructed in pUC19 (【75†26】), starting from plasmid pUC18ALA4 (【76†14】). Plasmids required for the complementation analysis of ssudeletions were constructed in pBluescript II KS, starting with plasmid pME4221, which carries the entire ssu operon on a 5.6-kbEcoRI fragment (【59†24】).\nL223: Growth of deletion mutants transformed with the appropriate plasmid was measured in 5 ml of sulfur-free M63 minimal medium supplemented with taurine or butanesulfonate as a source of sulfur. Cultures were incubated at 37°C overnight, and the optical density at 600 nm was recorded on the following day.\nL224: Alternatively, when growth of strains was investigated over a longer period of time, single colonies of the deletion mutant as well as of the strain transformed with the appropriate plasmid were resuspended in 50 μl of sulfur-free M63 minimal medium, from which 20 μl was spotted on an M63 minimal medium plate containing taurine or butanesulfonate as a sulfur source. Sulfur-limited solid media were prepared by the addition of 1.5 to 2% SeaPlaque agarose (FMC BioProducts).\nL225: Two plasmids that contain the ssu and tauregulatory sequences located upstream of the ssu andtau operons were constructed (【57†21】, 【59†24】). A 340-bpBamHI-EcoRI fragment from plasmid pME4204 (【59†24】) containing the ssu regulatory elements was ligated into pUC19, leading to plasmid pME4822.\nL226: For the construction of plasmid pME4821, which carries the tau regulatory sequences, a 450-bp BamHI-EcoRI fragment was PCR amplified from plasmid pUC18ALA4 (【76†14】) using primers JP7 and JP8 (【57†21】) and finally subcloned into pUC19.\nL227: ## RESULTS\nL228: ### Construction of in-frame deletions in the tau andssu operons.\nL229: Figure 【78†2】shows the functional transport components in deletion mutants that were constructed to probe for the requirement for the TauABC and SsuABC proteins for growth on alkanesulfonate compounds. A first group of mutants lacked one of the putative periplasmic binding proteins (Fig.【78†2】A and B) or the membrane-associated components TauBC or SsuCB (Fig. 【78†2】D and E). A second group included constructs that were defective in the corresponding components of both transport systems at the same time.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDeletion Analysis of the Escherichia coli Taurine and Alkanesulfonate Transport Systems | Journal of Bacteriology (https://journals.asm.org/doi/10.1128/jb.182.10.2687-2695.2000)\nciteturn31view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn29view1\",\"id\":57}); Total lines: 728\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn31view2 [wordlim: 200] Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn29view1\",\"id\":55}); \nFailed to fetch https://journals.asm.org/doi/reader/10.1128/jb.182.10.2687-2695.2000: Cache miss"
